          %title An informal introduction to topos theory
          %p
            %a#DocStart
          %p
            This is a changing, editable document. You can also read a
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/publications/published/Leinster2011"} peer-reviewed, unchanging, non-editable version
          %p
            As of 2022, the version below is not entirely readable: the diagrams and some other typesetting are broken. A
            %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20131212071554/http://ncatlab.org:80/publications/published/Leinster2011"} readable copy of the peer-reviewed, non-editable version
            (give the scripts a few seconds to load after opening the page) can be found in the Internet Archive.
          %h1#title An informal introduction to topos theory
          %h5#author Tom Leinster
          .thanks
            %p School of Mathematics and Statistics, University of Glasgow, Glasgow G12 8QW, UK; Tom.Leinster@glasgow.ac.uk. Supported by an EPSRC Advanced Research Fellowship.
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#introduction"} Introduction
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#acknowledgements"} Acknowledgements
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_definition_of_topos"} The definition of topos
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#fact"} Fact
              %li
                %a{:href => "#toposes_and_set_theory"} Toposes and set theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#digression"} Digression
              %li
                %a{:href => "#toposes_and_geometry"} Toposes and geometry
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#sheaves"} Sheaves
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#geometric_morphisms"} Geometric morphisms
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#points"} Points
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#embeddings_and_grothendieck_toposes"} Embeddings and Grothendieck toposes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#locales"} Locales
              %li
                %a{:href => "#toposes_and_universal_algebra"} Toposes and universal algebra
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#aside"} Aside
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            This short text is for readers who are confident in basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            but know little or nothing about
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            theorists. I am no expert on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
          %p
            In keeping with the spirit of the talks, what follows is light on both detail and references. For the reader wishing for more, almost everything here is presented in respectable form in Mac Lane and Moerdijk’s very pleasant introduction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#citeMaMo"}> 1994
            ). Nothing here is new, not even the expository viewpoint (very loosely inspired by
            %a{:href => "#citeJohSE"} Johnstone
            (
            %a{:href => "#citeJohSE"}> 2003
            )).
          %p
            As a rough indication of the level of knowledge assumed, I will take it that you are totally comfortable with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+Lemma"} Yoneda Lemma
            and the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            or of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p
            Section 
            %a{:href => "#sectiona"} 1
            explains the definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            and other subjects. There are many more such connections than I will mention; I hope it is abundantly clear that these notes are, by design, a quick sketch of a large subject.
          %p
            Section 
            %a{:href => "#sectionb"} 2
            is on connections between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            that sticks closely to how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            are actually used in mathematics. This provides an appealing alternative to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %p
              %em
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                is a generalized
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
          %p
            Section 
            %a{:href => "#sectionc"} 3
            is on connections with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            (in a broad sense); there the thought is that
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %p
              %em
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                is a generalized
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
          %p
            Section 
            %a{:href => "#sectiond"} 4
            is on connections with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %p
              %em
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                is a generalized
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
          %p
            What this means is that there is one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            embodying the concept of ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"}> ring
            ’, another embodying the concept of ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"}> field
            ’, and so on. This is the story of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+toposes"} classifying toposes
          %div
            %p
              Sections 
              %a{:href => "#sectionb"}> 2
              –
              %a{:href => "#sectiond"} 4
              can be read in any order, except that ideally §
              %a{:href => "#sectionc"} 3
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"}> geometry
              ) should come before §
              %a{:href => "#sectiond"} 4
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"}> universal algebra
              ). You
              %em can
              read §
              %a{:href => "#sectiond"} 4
              without having read §
              %a{:href => "#sectionc"}> 3
              , but the price to pay is that the notion of ‘
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"}> geometric morphism
              ’—defined in §
              %a{:href => "#sectionc"} 3
              and used in §
              %a{:href => "#sectiond"}> 4
              —might seem rather mysterious.
          %div
          %p
            Algebraic geometers beware: the word ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"}> topos
            ’ is used by mathematicians in two slightly different senses, according to circumstance and culture. There are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Category"} Category
            theorists tend to use ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"}> topos
            ’ to mean ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"}> elementary topos
            ’ by default, although
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            are also important in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometer"} algebraic geometer
            says ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"}> topos
            ’, they almost certainly mean ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"}> Grothendieck topos
            ’ (what else?).
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+sheaves"} categories of sheaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            are slightly more general, and the definition is simpler. They are what I will emphasize here.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            are the subject of Section 
            %a{:href => "#sectionc"}> 3
            , and appear fleetingly elsewhere; but if you only want to learn about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+sheaves"} categories of sheaves
          %h5#acknowledgements Acknowledgements
          %p
            I thank Andrei Akhvlediani,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenia+Cheng"} Eugenia Cheng
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Gurski"} Nick Gurski
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"} Emily Riehl
            for their participation and encouragement. Aspects of Section 
            %a{:href => "#sectiond"} 4
            draw on a vaguely similar presentation of vaguely similar material by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"} Marco Grandis
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
