          %p In summary,
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %p
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                form a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Choice"} Choice
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            has many other elementary properties (such as the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            has exactly two
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
          %p
            But what is this thing called ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+category+of+sets"}> the category of sets
            ’? What do we have to assume about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            in order to prove that these properties hold?
          %p
            Many mathematicians do not like to be bothered with such questions, because they know that the standard answer will be something like ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            are anything satisfying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            of
            = succeed "\u2019\u2014and" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            is irrelevant to what they do, and prefer not to hear about it.
          %p
            The standard answer is
            = succeed "," do
              %em valid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            satisfying the properties above. But it may seem
            = succeed "," do
              %em irrelevant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            system: all the properties are suggested directly by the naive imagery of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            as a bag of dots.
          %p
            There is, however, another type of answer—and this was
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %p
              %em
                we take the properties above as our
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
          %p
            In other words, we do away with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            entirely, and ask instead that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Choice"} Choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            is any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            satisfying these
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            %em a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Category"} Category
            of Sets (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"}> ETCS
            ), stated in modern language. (See
            %a{:href => "#citeLawETCS"} Lawvere
            (
            %a{:href => "#citeLawETCS"}> 1964
            ), or
            %a{:href => "#citeLaRo"} Lawvere and Rosebrugh
            (
            %a{:href => "#citeLaRo"}> 2003
            ) for a good expository account.) It is nearly fifty years old, but still has not gained the currency it deserves, for reasons on which one can speculate.
          %div{:style => "margin-left: 2em"}
            %h5#digression Digression
            %p
              You might be thinking that this is circular: that this axiomatization of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              depends on the notion of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              depends on some notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/collection"} collection
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
              does not depend on the general notion of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              = succeed "\u2019" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %p
              To see this, we need to back up a bit. The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
              axiomatization of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              looks, informally, like this:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  there are some things called ‘
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"}> sets
                  ’
              %li
                %p
                  there is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
                  ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∈
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
                  ’ on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              %li
                %p
                  some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
                  hold.
            %p
              People seeing this (or the formal version) often ask certain questions. What does ‘some things’ mean? Do you mean that there is a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              = succeed "\u2019?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              = succeed "\u2019" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in Y
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
              or
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} false
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
              course, and I will not attempt to answer the questions except to say that there is an assumed common understanding of these terms. To hide behind jargon,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} first-order theory
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
              axiomatization of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              looks like this:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  there are some things called ‘
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"}> sets
                  ’
              %li
                %p
                  for each
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                  from
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  to
                  = succeed "\u2019" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %li
                %p
                  for each
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Z
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} binary operation
                  assigning to each pair of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi Y
                        %mo →
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        f\colon X \to Y, \quad g\colon Y \to Z
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ f\colon X \to Z
              %li
                %p
                  some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
                  hold.
            %p
              You can ask the same kind of logical questions as for
              = succeed "\u2014what" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
              = succeed "\u2019?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} binary operation
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} first-order theory
            %p
              Stated in this way, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
              begin by saying that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative"} associative
              and has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identities"} identities
              (so that
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              define a
              = succeed ");" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              exist, and there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal"} terminal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              (so that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+category+of+sets"} the category of sets
              has
              = succeed ");" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Choice"} Choice
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            %p Here ends the digression.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            axiomatizes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and membership, whereas
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            axiomatizes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
            is defined as a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times Y
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is defined as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal"} terminal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            that are so important in traditional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            is slightly stronger than
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            can also be deduced in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            than are made by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
          %p
            The technical relationship between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            is well understood. It is known exactly which fragment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalent"} equivalent
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            (namely, ‘bounded’ or ‘restricted’ Zermelo with
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Choice"} Choice
            %a{:href => "#citeMaMo"} Mac Lane and Moerdijk
            (
            %a{:href => "#citeMaMo"}> 1994
            )). It is also known what needs to be added to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            in order to obtain a system of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equal"} equal
            strength to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            scheme (a countably infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family"} family
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            theorists in the traditional mould would call Replacement, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            theorists would call a form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocompleteness
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family"} family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_{i})_{i \in I}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            specified by a first-order formula, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum _{i \in I} X_{i}
            exists. The existence of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            is expressed by saying that there exist a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon X \to I
            (to be thought of as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum _{i \in I} X_{i} \to I
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{-1}\{ i\}
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{i}
            %a{:href => "#citeMcLECS"} McLarty
            (
            %a{:href => "#citeMcLECS"}> 2004
            ) for details.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
            theory therefore provides a different viewpoint on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed "-theoretic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
          %p
            Temporarily, let us say that a ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"}> category of sets
            ’ is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Choice"} Choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            scheme of Replacement. A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            is said to
            %strong
              %em{:style => "font-style: normal"} satisfy the Continuum Hypothesis
            if for all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mtext there exist monos
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi N
                          %mo ↣
                          %mi X
                          %mo ↣
                          %msup
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇒
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                          %mo ≅
                          %mi N
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtext or
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi X
                          %mo ≅
                          %msup
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \&amp;\text{there exist monos}\; N \rightarrowtail X \rightarrowtail 2^{N} \\ \implies &amp;X \cong N \;\text{or}\; X \cong 2^{N}.
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            (As usual,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            denotes the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            is the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of a first-order
            = succeed "\u2019," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
          %p
            So far we have seen the benefits of viewing the/a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            as a special
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            as a generalized
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Set}^{\mathbb{N}}
