            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            you can talk about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groups"} internal groups
            but not, in general,
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            you can talk about both. By extending the list of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/properties"} properties
            that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is assumed to satisfy, you can accommodate more and more sophisticated kinds of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            is more ‘sophisticated’ than that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            in the sense that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            is only defined for
            %em some
            pairs of maps, whereas classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            can only handle operations defined on
            %em all
            pairs.) The properties need not be of the form ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
